.th DISKTEST VIII
.dt
.sh NAME
disktest - direct access device diagnostic

.sh SYNOPSIS
disktest special-file

.sh DISCRIPTION
Disktest allows any direct access device (and some sequential devices)
to be tested while UNIX is still available to other users.  Care should
be taken to make the device which is to be tested unavailable to other
system users.  The single required argument is the name of the device which
is to be tested (ie. /dev/rk1).

Disktest recognizes the following single character commands some of which
may be followed by numeric arguments which are assumed to be octal
if they begin with a zero and decimal otherwise.

Command	Argument	Default		Meaning

  l		  1			  0			Low block number to test

  h		  1			  1024		High block number to test

  n		  0			  1			Number of blocks per transfer

  p		  1 or 2	  0			Pattern flag
									0  default patterns
									1  block number is pattern
									2  next argument is pattern

  o		  1			  1			Output flag
									0  output on errors only
									1  extended output

  d		  0			  -			Display test parameters

  t		  1			  1			Test repetition factor

  g		  0			  -			Execute write, read, compare test

  c		  0			  -			Execute a read, compare test

  r		  0			  -			Execute a read test

  w		  0			  -			Execute a write test

  q		  0			  -			Quit - return to the shell

  s		  1			  0			Sequential access
									0  sequential test
									1  random test

An interrupt (rubout) signal to disktest causes it to print the
current test number, block number, and pattern number.  A quit
signal causes disktest to abort the current test and await a new command.
Bits transferred per error are reported on rubout and at the end of
each test.
